    {
      "id": "cmsfkfuy5000104jozt2jbmvc",
      "term": "Walk me through the 3 financial statements & how they generally work",
      "setTitle": "Accounting - \"Talking\"",
      "klps": [
        {
          "text": "The income statement captures profitability over a period by netting expenses against revenue to reach net income.",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Net income is the starting point for the cash flow statement because it contains non-cash accruals.",
          "kind": "causal"
        },
        {
          "text": "Non-cash expenses like depreciation are added back to net income because they reduce accounting profit without an actual cash outflow.",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "The cash flow statement adjusts for changes in working capital and includes capital expenditures and financing activities to calculate the net change in cash.",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "The balance sheet represents a snapshot of the company's financial position at a specific date.",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "The fundamental accounting equation is that total assets must always equal the sum of liabilities and shareholders' equity.",
          "kind": "quantitative"
        },
        {
          "text": "Net income flows into the balance sheet through retained earnings, increasing shareholders' equity.",
          "kind": "causal"
        },
        {
          "text": "The ending cash balance from the cash flow statement is reported on the balance sheet as a current asset.",
          "kind": "causal"
        }
      ],
