    {
      "id": "cmsfkfv0w000d04jo3u3mgwm5",
      "term": "Walk me through the cash flow statement",
      "setTitle": "Accounting - \"Talking\"",
      "klps": [
        {
          "text": "The Cash Flow Statement is divided into three sections—Operating, Investing, and Financing activities—to reconcile Net Income to the net change in cash over a period.",
          "kind": "definition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Cash Flow from Operations starts with Net Income and adds back non-cash expenses like Depreciation & Amortization because they are accounting write-downs that do not actually reduce cash.",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "Cash Flow from Operations is adjusted for changes in operating working capital, where an increase in operating assets (like inventory or receivables) is a cash outflow, and an increase in operating liabilities (like accounts payable) is a cash inflow.",
          "kind": "causal"
        },
        {
          "text": "Cash Flow from Investing captures cash spent on or generated by long-term assets, primarily driven by Capital Expenditures, which represents cash outflows for physical property, plant, and equipment.",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "Cash Flow from Financing tracks cash transactions involving the raising or returning of capital, showing inflows from issuing debt or equity, and outflows from share repurchases, debt repayments, and dividend payments.",
          "kind": "mechanism"
        },
        {
          "text": "Summing the cash flows from the Operating, Investing, and Financing sections yields the Net Change in Cash, whereas Free Cash Flow is a distinct metric typically calculated as Operating Cash Flow minus Capital Expenditures.",
          "kind": "contrast"
        }
      ],
